  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nScattered showers and storms are ongoing and will continue into this \nevening. The overall threat of severe weather is quite low, but a \nfew strong storms are certainly still possible. The bigger threat to \nwatch out for through this evening will be localized flooding. The \nmain areas to watch will be east of I-75, especially across the East \nTN mountains and foothills, as well as southwest NC and VA. These \nare the areas that the HRRR have shown to have the best coverage of \nconvection through this evening. Any areas that see repeated rounds \nof convection will be at greatest risk to see flooding. \n\nMonday looks drier as we get some northerly flow in the mid to upper \nlevels on the backside of a weak shortwave. The only caveat is if \nan upstream MCS, across the Ohio River Valley, can propagate far \nenough south to make it into our area, mainly southwest VA. 12Z run \nof the HRRR shows a decaying MCS moving across southwest VA by late \nmorning/early afternoon. So this will be something to keep an eye \non, but otherwise drier for most areas on Monday.\n\nTuesday through Saturday features a more pronounced northwest flow \npattern across our area. This pattern during this time of year \ntypically favors increased chances of seeing an MCS across the area. \nWhile the pattern is conducive, predicting the when and the \nwhere are very difficult. Bottom line, increased chances for showers \nand storms Tuesday through Saturday. At some point during this \ntimeframe, at least one MCS, or a remnant, seems probable to impact \nour area. Stay tuned.\n\nBeyond Saturday, models are showing the ridge of high pressure \nshifting east. This takes us out of the northwest flow pattern and \nwould lead to drier and hotter conditions.",
